r hexagon is \\(\\boxed{42}\\) inches.", "The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is the sum of the lengths of its three sides. Given that the perimeter of one of the triangles is 21 inches, we can find the length of each side of the triangle by dividing the perimeter by 3.\n\nLet's denote the length of each side of the equilateral triangle as \\( s \\).\n\n\\[\ns = \\frac{\\text{Perimeter of the triangle}}{3} = \\frac{21}{3} = 7 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nSince a regular hexagon can be divided into six equilateral triangles, each side of the hexagon is equal to the side of one of these triangles. Therefore, the length of each side of the hexagon is also 7 inches.\n\nThe perimeter of a regular hexagon is the sum of the lengths of its six sides. So, we can calculate the perimeter of the hexagon as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter of the hexagon} = 6 \\times s = 6 \\times 7 = 42 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of the regular hexagon is \\(\\boxed{42}\\).", "Let's denote the three consecutive integers as \\(n-1\\), \\(n\\), and \\(n+1\\). The sum of these three integers is:\n\n\\[\n(n-1) + n + (n+1) = 3n\n\\]\n\nWe are looking for the smallest positive perfect cube that can be written in this form. So, we need \\(3n\\) to be a perfect cube. Let's denote the perfect cube by \\(k^3\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n3n = k^3\n\\]\n\nThis implies that \\(k^3\\) must be divisible by 3, and hence \\(k\\) must be divisible by 3. The smallest positive integer \\(k\\) that is divisible by 3 is 3 itself. So, let's substitute \\(k = 3\\):\n\n\\[\n3n = 3^3 = 27\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{27}{3} = 9\n\\]\n\nSo, the three consecutive integers are \\(8\\), \\(9\\), and \\(10\\), and their sum is \\(8 + 9 + 10 = 27\\), which is a perfect cube. Therefore, the smallest positive perfect cube that can be written as the sum of three consecutive integers is \\(27\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the s