\\) counter-clockwise.\n3. Translate the result back by adding \\( c \\).\n\nThe formula for rotating a complex number \\( z \\) around another complex number \\( c \\) by an angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by:\n\\[ w = (z - c) \\cdot e^{i\\theta} + c \\]\nwhere \\( e^{i\\theta} = \\cos(\\theta) + i\\sin(\\theta) \\).\n\nFor \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\), we have:\n\\[ e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\]\n\nLet's break this down step-by-step using Python and SymPy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex numbers z and c\nz = 2 + sp.sqrt(2) - (3 + 3*sp.sqrt(2))*sp.I\nc = 2 - 3*sp.I\n\n# Define the rotation angle\ntheta = sp.pi / 4\n\n# Calculate the rotation factor\nrotation_factor = sp.exp(sp.I * theta)\n\n# Translate z so that c becomes the origin\ntranslated_z = z - c\n\n# Rotate the translated z by theta\nrotated_z = translated_z * rotation_factor\n\n# Translate the result back by adding c\nw = rotated_z + c\n\n# Simplify the result\nw_simplified = sp.simplify(w)\n\nprint(w_simplified)\n```\n```output\n2 - 3*I + (1 + I)*(sqrt(2) + sqrt(2)*I)\n```\nThe output we got is \\(2 - 3i + (1 + i)(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}i)\\). Let's simplify this expression further.\n\nFirst, we need to expand the product \\((1 + i)(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}i)\\):\n\\[\n(1 + i)(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}i) = 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}i + i \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + i \\cdot \\sqrt{2}i = \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}i + \\sqrt{2}i + \\sqrt{2}i^2 = \\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{2}i - \\sqrt{2} = 2\\sqrt{2}i\n\\]\n\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n2 - 3i + 2\\sqrt{2}i = 2 + (2\\sqrt{2} - 3)i\n\\]\n\nThus, the complex number \\(w\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2 + (2\\sqrt{2} - 3)i}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to perform a rotation of the complex number \\( z \\) around the complex number \\( c \\) by \\( \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) radians counter-clockwise. Here are the steps to find \\(