s\ny = f(x)\n\n# Plot the function\nplt.plot(x, y, label='y = 2*sin(3x - pi) + 1')\nplt.xlabel('x')\nplt.ylabel('y')\nplt.title('Graph of y = 2*sin(3x - pi) + 1')\nplt.legend()\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()\n\n# The smallest possible value of c\nc = -np.pi\nprint(c)\n```\n```output\n-3.141592653589793\n```\nThe smallest possible value of \\( c \\) for the function \\( y = 2 \\sin(3x - \\pi) + 1 \\) is indeed \\( -\\pi \\), which is approximately \\(-3.141592653589793\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\pi}\n\\]", "To find the smallest possible value of \\( c \\) for the function \\( y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d \\), we need to analyze the given graph and determine the phase shift of the sine function.\n\nThe general form of the sine function is \\( y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d \\), where:\n- \\( a \\) is the amplitude,\n- \\( b \\) affects the period of the sine wave,\n- \\( c \\) is the phase shift, and\n- \\( d \\) is the vertical shift.\n\nFrom the graph, we can observe the following:\n1. The amplitude \\( a \\) is 2 because the maximum value of the function is 3 and the minimum value is -1. The amplitude is half the distance between the maximum and minimum values, so \\( a = \\frac{3 - (-1)}{2} = 2 \\).\n2. The vertical shift \\( d \\) is 1 because the midline of the function is at \\( y = 1 \\).\n3. The period of the sine wave is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{b} \\). From the graph, we can see that the period is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), so \\( b = 3 \\).\n4. The phase shift \\( c \\) can be determined by looking at the x-intercept or the starting point of the sine wave. The graph starts at \\( x = -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\), which means the sine function is shifted to the left by \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\). Therefore, the phase shift \\( c \\) is \\( -\\pi \\).\n\nHowever, since we are looking for the smallest possible value of \\( c \\) and the sine function is periodic with a period of \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), we can add or subtract multiples of \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\)