hon to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# First condition equation\r\neq1 = Eq((16 + 4*x) / 12, 4 / 3)\r\n\r\n# Second condition equation\r\neq2 = Eq((16 + x) / (12 - x), 4 / 3)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolution1 = solve(eq1, x)\r\nsolution2 = solve(eq2, x)\r\n\r\nprint((solution1, solution2))\n```\n```output\n([0.0], [0.666666666666667])\n```\nThe solutions to the equations are:\n\n1. From the first condition: \\( x = 0 \\)\n2. From the second condition: \\( x = \\frac{2}{3} \\)\n\nSince \\( x \\neq 0 \\), the valid solution is \\( x = \\frac{2}{3} \\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to set up the equations based on the given conditions and solve for \\(x\\).\n\n1. Let's denote the pay per dog as \\(p\\). Then, Denali's current pay is \\(16p\\) and Nate's current pay is \\(12p\\). The ratio of Denali's pay to Nate's pay is \\(\\frac{16p}{12p} = \\frac{4}{3}\\).\n\n2. According to the problem, this ratio would remain the same if Denali started walking \\(4x\\) more dogs and Nate stayed at 12 dogs. So, Denali's new number of dogs would be \\(16 + 4x\\) and Nate's number of dogs would remain \\(12\\). The new ratio of their pays would be \\(\\frac{(16 + 4x)p}{12p} = \\frac{16 + 4x}{12}\\). This ratio should be equal to \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\), so we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{16 + 4x}{12} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. According to the problem, the ratio would also remain the same if \\(x\\) of Nate's dogs were reassigned to Denali. So, Denali's new number of dogs would be \\(16 + x\\) and Nate's new number of dogs would be \\(12 - x\\). The new ratio of their pays would be \\(\\frac{(16 + x)p}{(12 - x)p} = \\frac{16 + x}{12 - x}\\). This ratio should also be equal to \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\), so we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{16 + x}{12 - x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\nLet's solve these equations step-by-step us