to set up the ratio of Denali's pay to Nate's pay in both scenarios and solve for \\(x\\).\n\nLet's denote the number of dogs Denali walks by \\(D\\) and the number of dogs Nate walks by \\(N\\). The ratio of their pay is the same as the ratio of the number of dogs they walk. So, initially, the ratio of Denali's pay to Nate's pay is \\(\\frac{16}{12} = \\frac{4}{3}\\).\n\nIn the first scenario, if Denali starts walking \\(4x\\) more dogs, then Denali will be walking \\(16 + 4x\\) dogs, and Nate will still be walking \\(12\\) dogs. The ratio of their pay in this scenario is \\(\\frac{16 + 4x}{12}\\).\n\nIn the second scenario, if \\(x\\) of Nate's dogs are reassigned to Denali, then Denali will be walking \\(16 + x\\) dogs, and Nate will be walking \\(12 - x\\) dogs. The ratio of their pay in this scenario is \\(\\frac{16 + x}{12 - x}\\).\n\nSince the ratio of Denali's pay to Nate's pay is the same in both scenarios, we can set up the following equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{16 + 4x}{12} = \\frac{16 + x}{12 - x}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this equation for \\(x\\) by cross-multiplying:\n\\[\n(16 + 4x)(12 - x) = 12(16 + x)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\\[\n192 + 48x - 16x - 4x^2 = 192 + 12x\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\\[\n192 + 32x - 4x^2 = 192 + 12x\n\\]\n\nSubtract 192 from both sides:\n\\[\n32x - 4x^2 = 12x\n\\]\n\nRearrange terms:\n\\[\n-4x^2 + 32x - 12x = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\\[\n-4x^2 + 20x = 0\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx(-4x + 20) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad -4x + 20 = 0\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x \\neq 0\\), we solve for the other solution:\n\\[\n-4x + 20 = 0 \\implies -4x = -20 \\implies x = 5\n\\]\n\nSo, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(5\\). Let's verify this solution by substituting \\(x = 5\\) back into the original equation to ensure it holds true.\n\nLet's use Python to verify this solution.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the ratio equality\r\ndef check_ratio(x):\r\n    # Initial ratio\r\n    initial_ratio = 16 / 