inal position? \\boxed{} \\boxed{15}\n\n10. A regular polygon with 30 sides is rotated counterclockwise about its center. What is the minimum number of degrees it must be rotated until it coincides with its original position? \\boxed{} \\boxed{12}', "The minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is \\(72^\\circ\\). This is because a regular pentagon has 5 sides, and a full rotation is \\(360^\\circ\\). When rotated, the pentagon will coincide with its original position after a rotation of \\(360^\\circ / 5 = 72^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code that calculates the minimum rotation angle for a regular pentagon.\n```python\n# Calculate the minimum rotation angle for a regular pentagon\nsides = 5\nfull_rotation = 360\nminimum_rotation = full_rotation / sides\nprint(minimum_rotation)\n```\n```output\n72.0\n```\nThe minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\).", "The minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is the same as the measure of one of its central angles. Since a regular pentagon has five equal sides and five equal angles, the central angle of a regular pentagon is \\( \\frac{360^\\circ}{5} = 72^\\circ \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code. We'll simply calculate the central angle of a regular pentagon.\n```python\n# Calculate the central angle of a regular pentagon\ncentral_angle = 360 / 5\nprint(central_angle)\n```\n```output\n72.0\n```\nThe minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\) degrees.", 'To solve this problem, we need to understand the symmetry of a regular pentagon. A regular pentagon has rotational symmetry of order 5, meaning it can be rotated by 72 degrees (360 degrees / 5) and still look the same. Therefore, the minimum number of degrees it must be rotated until it coincides with its original position is 72 degrees.\n\nHere is the Python code using sympy to confirm this:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the total degrees in a circle\ntotal_degrees = 360\n\n# Define the number of sides of the pentagon\nnum_sides = 5\n\n# Calculate the minimum degrees of rotation for the pentagon to coincide with its original position\nmin_rotation = total_degrees / num_sides\n\nprint(min_rotation)\n```\n```output\n72.0\n```\nThe minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated until it coincides with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\) degrees.', "The minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center to coincide with its original position can be determined by considering the symmetry of the pentagon. A regular pentagon has 5 sides and 5 rotational symmetries. The full rotation around the center of the pentagon is 360 degrees. The minimum angle of rotation that will bring the pentagon back to its original position is the angle that corresponds to one of these symmetries.\n\nSince a regular pentagon has 5 rotational symmetries, the minimum angle of rotation is \\( \\frac{360^\\circ}{5} = 72^\\circ \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code that calculates the minimum rotation angle.\n```python\n# Calculate the minimum rotation angle for a regular pentagon\nnum_sides = 5\nmin_rotation_angle = 360 / num_sides\nprint(min_rotation_angle)\n```\n```output\n72.0\n```\nThe minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center to coincide with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\) degrees.", 'To determine the minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position, we need to consider the symmetry of the pentagon.\n\nA regular pentagon has rotational symmetry. This means that it looks the same after being rotated by certain angles. The key angle of rotation for a regular pentagon is the angle that corresponds to the fraction of a full rotation (360 degrees) that brings one vertex to the position of another vertex.\n\nSince a regular pentagon has 5 vertices, it will coincide with its original position after being rotated by \\( \\frac{360^\\circ}{5} = 72^\\circ \\). This is because rotating the pentagon by 72 degrees will move each vertex to the position of the next vertex, and after 5 such rotations (which is a full rotation), the pentagon will be back in its original position.\n\nTherefore, the minimum number of degrees the pentagon must be rotated until it coincides with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\).', "The minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is the same as the central angle of the pentagon. A regular pentagon has 5 sides, so the central angle (the angle subtended by each side at the center) is \\( \\frac{360^\\circ}{5} = 72^\\circ \\). Therefore, the pentagon must be rotated by \\( 72^\\circ \\) to coincide with its original position.\n\nLet's confirm this with a small Python code snippet to ensure the calculation is accurate.\n```python\n# Calculate the central angle of a regular pentagon\nnum_sides = 5\ncentral_angle = 360 / num_sides\nprint(central_angle)\n```\n```output\n72.0\n```\nThe minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\) degrees.", 'A regular pentagon has five equal sides and five equal angles. When a regular pentagon is rotated about its center, it will coincide with its original position after a rotation of 360 degrees divided by the number of sides. In this case, the number of 