gree of \\((x-1) \\cdot f(x)\\) is 4, we can set up the equation \\( 1 + n = 4 \\). Solving for \\( n \\), we get \\( n = 3 \\). Thus, the degree of \\( f(x) \\) is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this by performing the polynomial division of \\( 3x^4 + x^3 - 25x^2 + 38x - 17 \\) by \\( x-1 \\) using Python. The quotient will be \\( f(x) \\), and its degree will confirm our answer.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the polynomials\nnumerator = 3*x**4 + x**3 - 25*x**2 + 38*x - 17\ndenominator = x - 1\n\n# Perform polynomial division\nquotient, remainder = sp.div(numerator, denominator)\n\n# Degree of the quotient\ndegree_of_quotient = sp.degree(quotient)\nprint((quotient, degree_of_quotient))\n```\n```output\n(3*x**3 + 4*x**2 - 21*x + 17, 3)\n```\nThe quotient obtained from the polynomial division is \\( 3x^3 + 4x^2 - 21x + 17 \\), and its degree is 3. This confirms that the degree of \\( f(x) \\) is indeed 3.\n\nThus, the degree of \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine the degree of the polynomial \\( f(x) \\), we need to analyze the given equation:\n\n\\[\n(x-1) \\cdot f(x) = 3x^4 + x^3 - 25x^2 + 38x - 17.\n\\]\n\nThe degree of the polynomial on the right-hand side is 4, as the highest power of \\( x \\) is \\( x^4 \\). On the left-hand side, the polynomial \\( (x-1) \\cdot f(x) \\) is the product of \\( (x-1) \\) and \\( f(x) \\). The degree of the product of two polynomials is the sum of their degrees. If the degree of \\( f(x) \\) is \\( n \\), then the degree of \\( (x-1) \\cdot f(x) \\) is \\( n + 1 \\). Since the degree of \\( (x-1) \\cdot f(x) \\) is 4, we have:\n\n\\[\nn + 1 = 4.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( n \\), we get:\n\n\\[\nn = 3.\n\\]\n\nSo, the degree of \\( f(x) \\) is 3. Let's verify this by performing polynomial division to find \\( f(x) \\).\n\nWe can use Python to perform the polynomial division and confirm the degree of \\( f(x) \\).\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx