^\\circ$. Each angle of the hexagon intercepts an arc that is twice the measure of the angle. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[2 \\cdot 105^\\circ + 2 \\cdot 110^\\circ + 2 \\cdot x + 2 \\cdot \\alpha + 2 \\cdot y + 2 \\cdot z = 360^\\circ.\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[210^\\circ + 220^\\circ + 2x + 2\\alpha + 2y + 2z = 360^\\circ.\\]\n\\[430^\\circ + 2(x + \\alpha + y + z) = 360^\\circ.\\]\n\\[2(x + \\alpha + y + z) = 360^\\circ - 430^\\circ.\\]\n\\[2(x + \\alpha + y + z) = -70^\\circ.\\]\n\nThis equation is not possible because the sum of the angles in a hexagon must be positive. Therefore,", "To solve the problem, we need to understand the properties of a cyclic hexagon and the angles subtended by the arcs of the circle. In a cyclic polygon, the sum of the interior angles is given by the formula \\((n-2) \\cdot 180^\\circ\\), where \\(n\\) is the number of sides. For a hexagon, this sum is \\((6-2) \\cdot 180^\\circ = 720^\\circ\\).\n\nThe given angles are \\(105^\\circ\\), \\(110^\\circ\\), and \\(\\alpha\\). The remaining three angles can be determined using the fact that the sum of the interior angles of the hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\). Let's denote the remaining three angles as \\(\\beta\\), \\(\\gamma\\), and \\(\\delta\\). So we have:\n\\[105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 720^\\circ.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[215^\\circ + \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 720^\\circ.\\]\nThus:\n\\[\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 505^\\circ.\\]\n\nSince the hexagon is cyclic, the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can pair the angles as follows:\n\\[\\alpha + \\delta = 180^\\circ \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta + \\gamma = 180^\\circ.\\]\nThis gives us:\n\\[\\alpha + \\delta = 180^\\circ \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta + \\gamma = 180^\\circ.\\]\nSo:\n\\[\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = \\alpha + (180^\\circ - \\gamma) + \\gamma + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 360^\\circ.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\\alpha + 