60 degrees\n# So, alpha + delta = 180 degrees and beta + gamma = 180 degrees\n# This gives us the remaining sum of angles as 145 degrees\n\n# Calculate alpha\nalpha = remaining_sum - (180 + 180 - angle1 - angle2)\n\nprint(alpha)\n```\n```output\n145\n```\nThe measure of \\(\\alpha\\) is \\(\\boxed{145}\\) degrees.", "To find the measure of the angle \\(\\alpha\\) in the inscribed hexagon, we can use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\). Since the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, the opposite angles will sum to \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nGiven:\n- One angle is \\(105^\\circ\\).\n- Another angle is \\(110^\\circ\\).\n- The angle \\(\\alpha\\) is unknown.\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of the hexagon is:\n\\[105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + \\alpha + \\text{three other angles} = 720^\\circ\\]\n\nSince the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\). Let's denote the other three angles as \\(\\beta\\), \\(\\gamma\\), and \\(\\delta\\). We can pair the angles as follows:\n\\[105^\\circ + \\beta = 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[110^\\circ + \\gamma = 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\alpha + \\delta = 180^\\circ\\]\n\nFrom these equations, we can solve for \\(\\beta\\), \\(\\gamma\\), and \\(\\delta\\):\n\\[\\beta = 180^\\circ - 105^\\circ = 75^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\gamma = 180^\\circ - 110^\\circ = 70^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\delta = 180^\\circ - \\alpha\\]\n\nNow we can substitute these values into the sum of the interior angles:\n\\[105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + \\alpha + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 720^\\circ\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side:\n\\[105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 720^\\circ\\]\n\\[540^\\circ = 720^\\circ - \\alpha\\]\n\nSo:\n\\[\\alpha = 720^\\circ - 540^\\circ = 180^\\circ\\]\n\nHowever, we need to check if this value is consistent with the given angles. Since the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), the correct value for \\(\\alpha\\) should be consistent with the given angles.