sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 + 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo, the solutions for \\(x\\) are \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\) and \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\).\n\nSimilarly, for \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\), we get the same quadratic equation for \\(x\\), and the solutions are \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\) and \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\).\n\nSo, the points of intersection are:\n\\[\n\\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to compute the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\). The distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is \\(\\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\\).\n\nFor the point \\(\\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right)\\), the distance to \\((-3, 2)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}} + 3\\right)^2 + \\left(10 + \\sqrt{41} - 2\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left(2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\right)^2 + \\left(8 + \\sqrt{41}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{4(9 + \\sqrt{41}) + (64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41)} = \\sqrt{36 + 4\\sqrt{41} + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{141 + 20\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nFor the point \\(\\left(-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right)\\), the distance to \\((-3, 2)\\) is the same as above, since the expression inside the square root is the same.\n\nFor the point \\(\\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right)\\), the distance to \\((-3, 2)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}} + 3\\right)^2 + \\left(10 - \\sqrt{41} - 2\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left(2\\sqrt{9 + \\