) + I)/(-1 + I*(1/137 + I*(1/137 + I*(1/137 + I*(1/137 + 2*I)/(-1 + I*(1/137 + I)) + I) + I) + I))\nz_2002: None\n```\nIt seems that the symbolic computation of the iterates is becoming quite complex. Instead of directly computing the iterates symbolically, we can simplify our approach by analyzing the function $F(z)$ and its properties.\n\nThe function $F(z) = \\frac{z + i}{z - i}$ is a Möbius transformation. Möbius transformations have the property that they can be periodic. Let\'s check if $F(z)$ is periodic by calculating a few iterates and looking for a pattern.\n\n1. **Calculate the first few iterates:**\n   - Start with $z_0 = \\frac{1}{137} + i$.\n   - Calculate $z_1 = F(z_0)$.\n   - Calculate $z_2 = F(z_1)$.\n   - Calculate $z_3 = F(z_2)$.\n   - Check if there is a periodic pattern.\n\n2. **Identify the periodicity:**\n   - If we find that $z_n = z_{n+k}$ for some $k$, then the sequence is periodic with period $k$.\n\n3. **Determine the value of $z_{2002}$:**\n   - Use the periodicity to find the value of $z_{2002}$.\n\nLet\'s simplify the calculation by using numerical evaluation to identify the periodicity.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the function F(z)\ndef F(z):\n    return (z + sp.I) / (z - sp.I)\n\n# Initial value z_0\nz_0 = sp.Rational(1, 137) + sp.I\n\n# Calculate the first few iterates to identify the periodicity\nz_1 = F(z_0).evalf()\nz_2 = F(z_1).evalf()\nz_3 = F(z_2).evalf()\nz_4 = F(z_3).evalf()\n\n# Print the first few iterates to identify the pattern\nprint(f"z_0: {z_0.evalf()}")\nprint(f"z_1: {z_1}")\nprint(f"z_2: {z_2}")\nprint(f"z_3: {z_3}")\nprint(f"z_4: {z_4}")\n\n# Check for periodicity\nif z_0.evalf() == z_4:\n    period = 4\nelse:\n    period = None\n\n# Determine z_2002 based on the periodicity\nif period is not None:\n    z_2002 = z_0 if 2002 % period == 0 else z_4\nelse:\n    z_2002 = None\n\nprint(f"z_2002: {z_2002}")\n```\n```output\nz_0: 1.02189781021898 + 1.0*I\nz_1: -1.37000000000000 + 1.0*I\nz_2: 1.02189781021