uence \\(z_n\\):**\n   Given \\(z_0 = \\frac{1}{137} + i\\), we need to compute \\(z_1 = F(z_0)\\), \\(z_2 = F(z_1)\\), and so on, to identify any patterns.\n\n3. **Identify the pattern:**\n   We need to determine if the sequence \\(z_n\\) is periodic, i.e., if there exists a positive integer \\(k\\) such that \\(z_{n+k} = z_n\\) for all \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Find \\(z_{2002}\\):**\n   If the sequence is periodic, we can use the periodicity to find \\(z_{2002}\\).\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python using sympy to perform the calculations.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex number z and the function F(z)\nz = sp.symbols(\'z\')\nF = (z + sp.I) / (z - sp.I)\n\n# Define the initial value z_0\nz_0 = sp.Rational(1, 137) + sp.I\n\n# Function to compute z_n\ndef compute_z_n(z_0, n):\n    z_n = z_0\n    for _ in range(n):\n        z_n = F.subs(z, z_n).simplify()\n    return z_n\n\n# Compute the first few terms to identify the pattern\nz_1 = compute_z_n(z_0, 1)\nz_2 = compute_z_n(z_0, 2)\nz_3 = compute_z_n(z_0, 3)\nz_4 = compute_z_n(z_0, 4)\n\nprint(f"z_0: {z_0}")\nprint(f"z_1: {z_1}")\nprint(f"z_2: {z_2}")\nprint(f"z_3: {z_3}")\nprint(f"z_4: {z_4}")\n\n# Check if the sequence is periodic\nif z_0 == z_4:\n    period = 4\nelse:\n    period = None\n\n# Find z_2002\nif period is not None:\n    z_2002 = compute_z_n(z_0, 2002 % period)\nelse:\n    z_2002 = compute_z_n(z_0, 2002)\n\nprint(f"z_2002: {z_2002}")\n```\n```output\nz_0: 1/137 + I\nz_1: I\nz_2: zoo\nz_3: zoo\nz_4: zoo\nz_2002: zoo\n```\nIt seems that the sequence \\(z_n\\) is not periodic with a period of 4, and it appears to diverge after the first few terms. Let\'s analyze the function \\(F(z)\\) more carefully to understand its behavior.\n\nGiven \\(F(z) = \\frac{z + i}{z - i}\\), let\'s compute the first few terms of the sequence manually to see if we can identify a pattern:\n\n1. \\(z_0 = \\frac{1}{137} + i\\)\n2. \\(z_1 = F(z_0) = \\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{137} + i\\right) + i}{\\left(\\frac{1}{137} +