n step by step.\n\n1. First, simplify the innermost expression \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}}\\).\n2. Then, take the cube root of the result from step 1.\n3. Finally, take the square root of the result from step 2.\n\nWe can use Python and the sympy library to perform these steps.\n\nHere is the Python code to simplify the expression:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = sp.sqrt(sp.root(sp.sqrt(1/729), 3))\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\n0.693361274350635\n```\nIt seems that the output is a numerical approximation. However, we need the exact simplified form with a rational denominator. Let's break down the expression step by step manually and then verify it with Python and sympy.\n\n1. Simplify the innermost expression \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{729}} = \\frac{1}{27}\n   \\]\n\n2. Take the cube root of \\(\\frac{1}{27}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{27}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{27}} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. Finally, take the square root of \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\nSo the simplified form of the expression is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = sp.sqrt(sp.root(sp.sqrt(1/729), 3))\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\n\n# Convert to rational form\nrational_expression = sp.nsimplify(simplified_expression)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(rational_expression)\n```\n```output\n693361274350635/1000000000000000\n```\nIt seems that the output is still a numerical approximation. Let's manually verify the steps and ensure that we get the exact simplified form with a rational denominator.\n\nWe'll break down the expression step by step again:\