tems to choose from, \\( r \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial, which is the product of all positive integers up to that number.\n\nIn this problem, \\( n = 8 \\) and \\( r = 2 \\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8!}{2!(8-2)!} = \\frac{8!}{2! \\cdot 6!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the factorials. Recall that \\( 8! = 8 \\times 7 \\times 6! \\). The \\( 6! \\) terms cancel out in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8 \\times 7 \\times 6!}{2! \\times 6!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7}{2!}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\( 2! \\), which is \\( 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\):\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8 \\times 7}{2} = \\frac{56}{2} = 28\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different pizzas Mikka can order is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{28}\n\\]', "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways Mikka can choose 2 toppings out of 8 available toppings. This is a combination problem where the order of selection does not matter.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\n\\[\nC(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\nIn this case, \\( n = 8 \\) (the total number of toppings) and \\( k = 2 \\) (the number of toppings Mikka wants to choose).\n\nLet's calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Total number of toppings\nn = 8\n\n# Number of toppings to choose\nk = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of combinations\ncombinations = math.comb(n, k)\nprint(combinations)\n```\n```output\n28\n```\nThe number of different pizzas Mikka could order with two different toppings from a selection of 8 toppings is \\(\\boxed{28}\\).", 'To determine how many different pizzas Mikka can order with two different toppings from a choice of 8 toppings, we need to calculate the number of combinations of 8 toppings taken 2 at a time. This is because the order in which the toppings are chosen does not matter.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, \\( r \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial, which is the product of all positive integers up to that number.\n\nIn this problem, \\( n = 8 \\) and \\( r = 2 \\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8!}{2!(8-2)!} = \\frac{8!}{2!6!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the factorials. Recall that \\( 8! = 8 \\times 7 \\times 6! \\). The \\( 6! \\) terms cancel out in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8 \\times 7 \\times 6!}{2! \\times 6!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7}{2!}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\( 2! \\), which is \\( 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\):\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8 \\times 7}{2} = \\frac{56}{2} = 28\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different pizzas Mikka can order is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{28}\n\\]\n\nFind the roots of the quadratic polynomial $f(x) = -\\frac{59}{5}x^2 + \\frac{64}{5}x - \\frac{57}{5}$.\nTo find the roots of the quadratic polynomial \\( f(x) = -\\frac{59}{5}x^2 + \\frac{64}{5}x - \\frac{57}{5} \\), we will use the quadratic formula, which is given by:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\n\nHere, the coefficients \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are:\n\\[\na = -\\frac{59}{5}, \\quad b = \\frac{64}{5}, \\quad c = -\\frac{57}{5}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant \\(\\Delta\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = \\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right)^2 - 4 \\left(-\\frac{59}{5}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{57}{5}\\right)\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right)^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right)^2 = \\frac{64^2}{5^2} = \\frac{4096}{25}\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(4 \\left(-\\frac{59}{5}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{57}{5}\\right)\\):\n\n\\[\n4 \\left(-\\frac{59}{5}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{57}{5}\\right) = 4 \\cdot \\frac{59 \\cdot 57}{25} = \\frac{4 \\cdot 3363}{25} = \\frac{13452}{25}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values back into the discriminant formula:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = \\frac{4096}{25} - \\frac{13452}{25} = \\frac{4096 - 13452}{25} = \\frac{-9356}{25}\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) is negative (\\(\\Delta = \\frac{-9356}{25}\\)), the quadratic equation has no real roots. Instead, it has two complex roots.\n\nTo find the complex roots, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{\\Delta}}{2a}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(b = \\frac{64}{5}\\), \\(\\Delta = \\frac{-9356}{25}\\), and \\(a = -\\frac{59}{5}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-\\frac{64}{5} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-9356}{25}}}{2 \\left(-\\frac{59}{5}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the square root of the negative discriminant:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{-9356}{25}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{-9356}}{\\sqrt{25}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{9356}i}{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt{9356} = 96.73\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{-9356}{25}} = \\frac{96.73i}{5} = 19.346i\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute back into the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-\\frac{64}{5} \\pm 19.346i}{2 \\left(-\\frac{59}{5}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the denominator:\n\n\\[\n2 \\left(-\\frac{59}{5}\\right) = -\\frac{118}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-\\frac{64}{5} \\pm 19.346i}{-\\frac{118}{5}} = \\frac{-64 \\pm 96.73i}{-118}\n\\]\n\nSeparate the real and imaginary parts:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-64}{-118} \\pm \\frac{96.73i}{-118}\n\\]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{64}{118} \\mp \\frac{96.73i}{118}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{32}{59} \\mp \\frac{48.365i}{59}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the roots of the quadratic polynomial are:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{32}{59} - \\frac{48.365i}{59} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{32}{59} + \\frac{48.365i}{59}\n\\]\n\nIn simplified form, the roots are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{32}{59} \\pm \\frac{48.365i}{59}}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the cross product of the following vectors:\n\nVector A: $\\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\frac{19}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n-\\frac{13}{2} \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right)$\n\nVector B: $\\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\n-\\frac{17}{2} \\\\\n-9 \\\\\n-9 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right)$\nTo calculate the cross product of vectors \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{B}\\), we use the determinant of a matrix formed by the unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{i}\\), \\(\\mathbf{j}\\), \\(\\mathbf{k}\\), and the components of the vectors \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{B}\\). The cross product \\(\\mathbf{A} \\times \\mathbf{B}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\times \\mathbf{B} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n\\frac{19}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{13}{2} \\\\\n-\\frac{17}{2} & -9 & -9 \\\\\n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe expand this determinant along the first row:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\times \\mathbf{B} = \\mathbf{i} \\left( \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot (-9) - \\left( -\\frac{13}{2} \\right) \\cdot (-9) \\right) - \\mathbf{j} \\left( \\frac{19}{2} \\cdot (-9) - \\left( -\\frac{13}{2} \\right) \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{17}{2} \\right) \\right) + \\mathbf{k} \\left( \\frac{19}{2} \\cdot (-9) - \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{17}{2} \\right) \\right)\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate each component separately.\n\nFor the \\(\\mathbf{i}\\)-component:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{i} \\left( \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot (-9) - \\left( -\\frac{13}{2} \\right) \\cdot (-9) \\right) = \\mathbf{i} \\left( -\\frac{45}{2} - \\frac{117}{2} \\right) = \\mathbf{i} \\left( -\\frac{162}{2} \\right) = \\mathbf{i} \\left( -81 \\right) = -81\\mathbf{i}\n\\]\n\nFor the \\(\\mathbf{j}\\)-component:\n\n\\[\n-\\mathbf{j} \\left( \\frac{19}{2} \\cdot (-9) - \\left( -\\frac{13}{2} \\right) \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{17}{2} \\right) \\right) = -\\mathbf{j} \\left( -\\frac{171}{2} - \\frac{221}{4} \\right) = -\\mathbf{j} \\left( -\\frac{342}{4} - \\frac{221}{4} \\right) = -\\mathbf{j} \\left( -\\frac{563}{4} \\right) = \\frac{563}{4}\\mathbf{j}\n\\]\n\nFor the \\(\\mathbf{k}\\)-component: