[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{256}}\n\\]", "To find the value of \\(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ\\), we can use trigonometric identities and symmetry properties. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n1. First, we note that \\(\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n2. We can pair the angles in a way that allows us to use product-to-sum identities or symmetry properties. Notice that \\(\\sin 100^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\sin 80^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 40^\\circ) = \\sin 40^\\circ\\). Similarly, \\(\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ\\).\n\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = (\\sin 20^\\circ)^3 (\\sin 40^\\circ)^2 (\\sin 60^\\circ) (\\sin 80^\\circ)^2.\n\\]\n\nWe can further simplify using the known value of \\(\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\):\n\\[\n(\\sin 20^\\circ)^3 (\\sin 40^\\circ)^2 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) (\\sin 80^\\circ)^2.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the values of \\(\\sin 20^\\circ\\), \\(\\sin 40^\\circ\\), and \\(\\sin 80^\\circ\\). We can use the fact that the product of sines of angles in arithmetic progression can be simplified using complex numbers or roots of unity. However, for simplicity, we can use the known result that the product of sines of angles in arithmetic progression is given by:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1} \\sin \\left(\\frac{k\\pi}{n}\\right) = \\frac{n}{2^{n-1}}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 9\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{9} \\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{9} \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{9} \\sin \\frac{4\\pi}{9} \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{9} \\sin \\frac{6\\pi}{9} \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{9} \\sin \\frac{8\\pi}{9} = \\frac{9}{2^8} = \\frac{9}{256}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin \\frac{k\\pi}{9} = \\sin 20^\\circ, \\sin 40^\\circ, \\sin 60^\\circ, \