 values will be zero only if both expressions inside the absolute values are zero simultaneously. This happens at \\(x = -4\\).\n\nTherefore, the only value of \\(x\\) that makes the denominator zero is \\(x = -4\\).\n\nSo, the value of \\(x\\) that is not in the domain of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(x = -4\\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\ndenominator = sp.Abs(x**2 + 3*x - 4) + sp.Abs(x**2 + 9*x + 20)\n\n# Solve for the values of x that make the denominator zero\nsolutions = sp.solve(denominator, x)\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\nNotImplementedError: solving Abs(x**2 + 9*x + 20) when the argument is not real or imaginary.\n```\nIt seems that the direct approach using `sympy.solve` for absolute values is not straightforward. Instead, we can manually check the values that make each absolute value expression inside the denominator zero and verify that the sum of the absolute values is zero only at \\(x = -4\\).\n\nLet's manually verify this by evaluating the expressions at the critical points \\(x = -4\\), \\(x = 1\\), and \\(x = -5\\).\n\n1. At \\(x = -4\\):\n\\[|(-4)^2 + 3(-4) - 4| + |(-4)^2 + 9(-4) + 20| = |16 - 12 - 4| + |16 - 36 + 20| = |0| + |0| = 0\\]\n\n2. At \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[|1^2 + 3(1) - 4| + |1^2 + 9(1) + 20| = |1 + 3 - 4| + |1 + 9 + 20| = |0| + |30| = 30 \\neq 0\\]\n\n3. At \\(x = -5\\):\n\\[|(-5)^2 + 3(-5) - 4| + |(-5)^2 + 9(-5) + 20| = |25 - 15 - 4| + |25 - 45 + 20| = |6| + |0| = 6 \\neq 0\\]\n\nFrom the above evaluations, we can see that the only value of \\(x\\) that makes the denominator zero is \\(x = -4\\). Therefore, the value of \\(x\\) that is not in the domain of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(x = -4\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-4}\n\\]", "To determine the values of \\( x \\) that are not in the domain of the function \n\n\\[ f(x) = \\frac{1}{|x^2 + 3x - 4| + |x^2 + 9x + 20|}, \\]\n\nwe need to identify the points where the denominator is zero.