ty theorem, the following must be true:\n\n1. \\(1 + 3 > x\\) or \\(4 > x\\) or \\(x < 4\\)\n2. \\(1 + x > 3\\) or \\(x > 2\\)\n3. \\(3 + x > 1\\) or \\(x > -2\\) (which is always true for positive \\(x\\))\n\nCombining these inequalities, we get \\(2 < x < 4\\). Since \\(x\\) is an integer, the only possible value for \\(x\\) is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the sides of the triangle\nside1 = 1\nside2 = 3\n\n# Calculate the possible range for the third side\nmin_third_side = side2 - side1 + 1  # Minimum value for the third side\nmax_third_side = side1 + side2 - 1  # Maximum value for the third side\n\n# Find the integer value for the third side\npossible_third_sides = [x for x in range(min_third_side, max_third_side + 1) if x > 0]\n\n# Since we know there should be only one possible integer value, we can print the first (and only) element of the list\nthird_side = possible_third_sides[0]\nprint(third_side)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe length of the third side of the triangle is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine the length of the third side of a triangle when the lengths of the other two sides are known, we can use the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nGiven that the lengths of two sides of the triangle are 1 unit and 3 units, let's denote the third side by \\(x\\). The conditions for \\(x\\) to form a valid triangle with 1 and 3 are:\n\n1. \\(1 + 3 > x\\) ⟹ \\(4 > x\\) ⟹ \\(x < 4\\)\n2. \\(1 + x > 3\\) ⟹ \\(x > 2\\)\n3. \\(3 + x > 1\\) ⟹ \\(x > -2\\) (which is always true for positive \\(x\\))\n\nCombining these conditions, we get \\(2 < x < 4\\). Since \\(x\\) is an integer, the only possible value for \\(x\\) is 3.\n\nThus, the length of the third side is \\(3\\) units.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the lengths of the t