144 \\ge 54p\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{144}{54} \\ge p\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{8}{3} \\ge p\n\\]\n\\[\np \\le \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Solve the second inequality \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\)\n\n\\[\n0 > 12 - 20p\n\\]\n\\[\n20p > 12\n\\]\n\\[\np > \\frac{12}{20}\n\\]\n\\[\np > \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the intersection of the solutions\n\nThe first inequality gives us \\( p \\le \\frac{8}{3} \\) and the second inequality gives us \\( p > \\frac{3}{5} \\). The intersection of these two intervals is:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer in interval notation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right]}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure the solution is accurate.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, solve, S\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\np = symbols('p')\r\n\r\n# Define the inequalities\r\nineq1 = 0 >= 54*p - 144\r\nineq2 = 0 > 12 - 20*p\r\n\r\n# Solve the inequalities\r\nsol1 = solve(ineq1, p)\r\nsol2 = solve(ineq2, p)\r\n\r\n# Find the intersection of the solutions\r\nsolution = S.Intersection(S.Interval.open(3/5, float('inf')), S.Interval(-float('inf'), 8/3))\r\nprint(solution)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to find the values of \\( p \\) that satisfy both inequalities:\n\n1. \\( 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\)\n2. \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\)\n\nLet's solve each inequality separately and then find the intersection of the solutions.\n\n### Solving the first inequality \\( 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\):\n\n\\[\n0 \\ge 54p - 144\n\\]\n\\[\n144 \\ge 54p\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{144}{54} \\ge p\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{8}{3} \\ge p\n\\]\n\\[\np \\le \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Solving the second inequality \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\):\n\n\\[\n0 > 12 - 20p\n\\]\n\\[\n20p > 12\n\\]\n\\[\np > \\frac{12}{20}\n\\]\n\\[\np > \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\n### Finding the intersection of the solutions:\n\nThe first inequality gives \\( p \\le \\frac{8}{3} \\) and the second inequality gives \\( p > \\frac{3}{5} \\). Therefore, the values of \\( p \