ts of employees numbered 21 to 25. Since 22 is drawn from this group, the starting point of the sampling sequence must be 2 (because 22 - 5*(5-1) = 2).\n\nNow, to find the number drawn from the 10th group, we need to calculate the position in the sequence. The 10th group consists of employees numbered 46 to 50. The number drawn from this group will be the starting point (2) plus 9 times the sampling interval (5), which is 2 + 9*5 = 47.\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this:\n\n```python\n# Define the starting point and the sampling interval\nstarting_point = 2\nsampling_interval = 5\n\n# Calculate the number drawn from the 10th group\ngroup_number = 10\nnumber_drawn = starting_point + (group_number - 1) * sampling_interval\n\nprint(number_drawn)\n```\n```output\n47\n```\nThe number drawn from the 10th group should be \\(\\boxed{47}\\).\n\nThe set $S = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots , 49, 50\\}$ contains the first $50$ positive integers.  After the multiples of 2 and the multiples of 3 are removed, how many integers remain in the set $S$?\nTo determine the number of integers remaining in the set \\( S = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots , 49, 50\\} \\) after removing the multiples of 2 and multiples of 3, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify and count the multiples of 2 within the set \\( S \\).\n2. Identify and count the multiples of 3 within the set \\( S \\).\n3. Identify and count the multiples of both 2 and 3 (which are multiples of 6) to avoid double-counting.\n4. Apply the principle of inclusion-exclusion to find the total number of elements to be removed.\n5. Subtract the total number of elements to be removed from the total number of elements in \\( S \\) to get the final count of remaining integers.\n\nLet's proceed step-by-step using Python code for accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the set S\nS = set(range(1, 51))\n\n# Identify multiples of 2\nmultiples_of_2 = {i for i in range(2, 51, 2)}\n\n# Identify multiples of 3\nmultiples_of_3 = {i for i in range(3, 51, 3)}\n\n# 