 6) + (4 \\times 6) + (4 \\times 4) + (9 \\times 5)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Total number of stamps} = 12 + 4 + 4 + 9\n\\]\n\nFinally, we will compute the average price per stamp.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average price} = \\frac{\\text{Total cost}}{\\text{Total number of stamps}}\n\\]\n\nLet\'s use Python to perform these calculations.\n```python\n# Define the number of stamps and their respective costs\nbrazil_stamps = 12\nfrance_stamps = 4\nperu_stamps = 4\nspain_stamps = 9\n\nbrazil_cost = 6\nfrance_cost = 6\nperu_cost = 4\nspain_cost = 5\n\n# Calculate the total cost for each country\ntotal_cost_brazil = brazil_stamps * brazil_cost\ntotal_cost_france = france_stamps * france_cost\ntotal_cost_peru = peru_stamps * peru_cost\ntotal_cost_spain = spain_stamps * spain_cost\n\n# Calculate the total cost and total number of stamps\ntotal_cost = total_cost_brazil + total_cost_france + total_cost_peru + total_cost_spain\ntotal_stamps = brazil_stamps + france_stamps + peru_stamps + spain_stamps\n\n# Calculate the average price per stamp\naverage_price = total_cost / total_stamps\n\n# Round the result to the nearest tenth of a cent\naverage_price_rounded = round(average_price, 1)\nprint(average_price_rounded)\n```\n```output\n5.4\n```\nThe average price of Juan\'s 70\'s stamps, rounded to the nearest tenth of a cent, is \\(\\boxed{5.4}\\) cents.', '$\\textbf{Juan\'s Old Stamping Grounds}$\n\nJuan organizes the stamps in his collection by country and by the decade in which they were issued. The prices he paid for them at a stamp shop were: Brazil and France, $6$ cents each, Peru $4$ cents each, and Spain $5$ cents each. (Brazil and Peru are South American countries and France and Spain are in Europe.) [asy]\n/* AMC8 2002 #8, 9, 10 Problem */\nsize(3inch, 1.5inch);\nfor ( int y = 0; y <= 5; ++y )\n{\n\ndraw((0,y)--(18,y));\n}\ndraw((0,0)--(0,5));\ndraw((6,0)--(6,5));\ndraw((9,0)--(9,5));\ndraw((12,0)--(12,5));\ndraw((15,0)--(15,5));\ndraw((18,0)--(18,5));\n\nlabel(scale(0.8)*"50s", (7.5,4.5))