fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$, we can substitute $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$ into the operation. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify $\\frac{21}{30}$. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so we can simplify $\\frac{21}{30}$ to $\\frac{7}{10}$. Thus, we have:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is 49. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Define the fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Simplify q/n\nsimplified_q_n = q / n\n\n# Calculate the result of the operation\nresult = m * p * simplified_q_n\n\n# Since the result is an integer, we can directly print it\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49.0\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to apply the operation @ as defined. The operation is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFor the given fractions \\(\\frac{7}{30}\\) and \\(\\frac{10}{21}\\), we can substitute \\(m = 7\\), \\(n = 30\\), \\(p = 10\\), and \\(q = 21\\). So the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to simplify \\(\\frac{21}{30}\\). The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{21 \\div 3}{30 \\div 3} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute this back into the expression:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 10 \\cdot \\frac{7}{10} = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of \\(\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}\\) is \\(49\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\r\n\r\n# Define the fractions\r\nm, n = 7, 30\r\np, q = 10