hus, \\( p(x) \\) is a constant polynomial, and its degree is 0.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]", "To determine the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\), we need to analyze the expression given:\n\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify the polynomial by examining each term individually. Each term in the polynomial is a quadratic polynomial divided by a constant. The degree of each term is determined by the degree of the numerator, since the denominator is a constant.\n\n1. The first term \\(\\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)}\\) is a quadratic polynomial divided by a constant, so it is a polynomial of degree 2.\n2. The second term \\(\\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)}\\) is also a quadratic polynomial divided by a constant, so it is a polynomial of degree 2.\n3. The third term \\(\\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}\\) is a quadratic polynomial divided by a constant, so it is a polynomial of degree 2.\n\nSince each term in the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is of degree 2, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is the maximum degree of its terms, which is 2.\n\nHowever, we should verify if there is any simplification that might reduce the degree of the polynomial. Let's evaluate \\( p(x) \\) at specific points to see if there is any pattern or simplification.\n\n1. Evaluate \\( p(a) \\):\n\\[ p(a) = \\frac{(a - b)(a - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\n2. Evaluate \\( p(b) \\):\n\\[ p(b) = \\frac{(b - b)(b - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\n3. Evaluate \\( p(c) \\):\n\\[ p(c) = \\frac{(c - b)(c - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1. \\]\n\nFrom the evaluations, we see that \\( p(a) = 1 \\), \\( p(b) = 1 \\), and \\( p(c) = 1 \\). This suggests that the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is a constant polynomial equal to 1. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is 0.\n\nLet's confirm this by simplifying the polynomial algebraically. We can use Python to verify this result.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nx, a, b, c = symbols('x a b c')\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial p(x)\r\np_x = ((x - b)*(x - c))/((a - b)*(a - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - c))/((b - a)*(b - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - b))/((c - a)*(c - b))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the polynomial\r\np_x_simplified = simplify(p_x)\r\nprint(p_x_simplified)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe simplified form of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\( 1 \\), which is a constant polynomial. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\( 0 \\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]", "To determine the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\), we need to analyze the expression:\n\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a more convenient form. Notice that the terms in the numerator are quadratic polynomials, and the denominators are constants. Therefore, \\( p(x) \\) is a sum of three quadratic polynomials divided by constants. This means that \\( p(x) \\) is itself a polynomial of degree at most 2.\n\nTo confirm this, let's evaluate \\( p(x) \\) at specific points. If we evaluate \\( p(x) \\) at \\( x = a \\), \\( x = b \\), and \\( x = c \\), we should get specific values that will help us determine the polynomial.\n\n1. Evaluate \\( p(a) \\):\n\\[ p(a) = \\frac{(a - b)(a - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\n2. Evaluate \\( p(b) \\):\n\\[ p(b) = \\frac{(b - b)(b - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - c)}{(b -