ulation.\n\n```python\nimport itertools\nimport random\n\ndef do_segments_intersect(points):\n    A, B, C, D = points\n    # Check if the points are in alternating order\n    if (A < B < C < D) or (A < C < B < D) or (A < D < B < C) or \\\n       (B < A < C < D) or (B < C < A < D) or (B < D < A < C) or \\\n       (C < A < B < D) or (C < B < A < D) or (C < D < A < B) or \\\n       (D < A < B < C) or (D < B < A < C) or (D < C < A < B):\n        return True\n    return False\n\ndef simulate_probability(num_trials):\n    count_intersections = 0\n    for _ in range(num_trials):\n        points = list(range(4))\n        random.shuffle(points)\n        if do_segments_intersect(points):\n            count_intersections += 1\n    return count_intersections / num_trials\n\n# Run the simulation with a large number of trials\nnum_trials = 100000\nprobability = simulate_probability(num_trials)\nprint(probability)\n```\n\nThe output of the simulation should be close to the theoretical probability of $\\frac{1}{4}$. Let's run the code and confirm the result.\n```python\nimport itertools\nimport random\n\ndef do_segments_intersect(points):\n    A, B, C, D = points\n    # Check if the points are in alternating order\n    if (A < B < C < D) or (A < C < B < D) or (A < D < B < C) or \\\n       (B < A < C < D) or (B < C < A < D) or (B < D < A < C) or \\\n       (C < A < B < D) or (C < B < A < D) or (C < D < A < B) or \\\n       (D < A < B < C) or (D < B < A < C) or (D < C < A < B):\n        return True\n    return False\n\ndef simulate_probability(num_trials):\n    count_intersections = 0\n    for _ in range(num_trials):\n        points = list(range(4))\n        random.shuffle(points)\n        if do_segments_intersect(points):\n            count_intersections += 1\n    return count_intersections / num_trials\n\n# Run the simulation with a large number of trials\nnum_trials = 100000\nprobability = simulate_probability(num_trials)\nprint(probability)\n```\n```output\n1.0\n```\nIt seems the