) = 1.\n\\]\nAnd:\n\\[\n(x + y)(y + z) = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}} + \\sqrt[4]{2}\\right) \\left(\\sqrt[4]{2} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}} + \\sqrt[4]{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\right)^2 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2} + \\left(\\sqrt[4]{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + 2 + \\sqrt{2} = 2 + \\sqrt{2} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = 2 + \\sqrt{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 2 + \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\nThis value is not the minimum. Let's try another approach. We can use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value. Let's set up the Lagrangian:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L} = (x + y)(y + z) + \\lambda \\left(1 - xyz(x + y + z)\\right).\n\\]\nTaking the partial derivatives and setting them to zero, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x} = y + z - \\lambda yz(x + y + z) - \\lambda xyz = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial y} = x + z + x + z - \\lambda xz(x + y + z) - \\lambda xyz = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial z} = x + y - \\lambda xy(x + y + z) - \\lambda xyz = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\lambda} = 1 - xyz(x + y + z) = 0.\n\\]\nSolving these equations, we find that the minimum value of \\((x + y)(y + z)\\) is indeed 2, which occurs when \\(x = z = 1\\) and \\(y = \\frac{1}{2}\\). Let's verify this:\n\\[\nxyz(x + y + z) = 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{2} + 1\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} = \\frac{5}{4} \\neq 1.\n\\]\nThis means we need to find another set of values for \\(x, y, z\\) that satisfy both conditions. Let's try \\(x = z = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\) and \\(y = \\sqrt[4]{2}\\). Then:\n\\[\nxyz(x + y + z) = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\right) \\left(\\sqrt[4]{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}} + \\sqrt[4]{2} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqr