 to find the integer part \\(a\\) of \\(\\sqrt[3]{2}\\). Since \\(1 < \\sqrt[3]{2} < 2\\), we have \\(a = 1\\). Now we need to find the continued fraction representation of \\(\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\\) by \\(x\\). Then:\n\n\\[\nx = \\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\n\\]\n\nWe can express \\(\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) in terms of \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{2} = x + 1\n\\]\n\nCubing both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n2 = (x + 1)^3 = x^3 + 3x^2 + 3x + 1\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 3x^2 + 3x - 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the continued fraction representation of \\(x\\). We can use the method of successive approximations to find the continued fraction representation. Let's start by finding the integer part of \\(1/x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{2} - 1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1} = \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1}{2 - 1} = \\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2 < \\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} + 1 < 3\\), the integer part is \\(b = 2\\). Now we need to find the continued fraction representation of \\(\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\\) by \\(y\\). Then:\n\n\\[\ny = \\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 1\n\\]\n\nWe can express \\(\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2}\\) in terms of \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} = y + 1\n\\]\n\nCubing both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n4 + 3\\sqrt[3]{8} + 3\\sqrt[3]{4} + 2 = (y + 1)^3 = y^3 + 3y^2 + 3y + 1\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt[3]{8} = 2\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n6 + 3\\sqrt[3]{4} + 3\\sqrt[3]{2} = y^3 + 3y^2 + 3y + 1\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\ny^3 + 3y^2 + 3y - 3\\sqrt[3]{4} - 3\\sqrt[3]{2} - 5 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis equation is more complex, but we can use the method of successive approximations to find the continued fraction repr