_n(decimal_number, base):\n    if decimal_number == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    digits = []\n    while decimal_number:\n        digits.append(int(decimal_number % base))\n        decimal_number //= base\n    \n    # The remainders give the digits of the number in reverse order\n    digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(map(str, digits))\n\n# Convert 999 from base 10 to base 6\nbase_6_representation = decimal_to_base_n(999, 6)\nprint(base_6_representation)\n```\n```output\n4343\n```\nThe base six equivalent of \\(999_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{4343_6}\\).', 'To convert a decimal number to its base six equivalent, we repeatedly divide the number by 6 and keep track of the remainders. The base six representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top. Let\'s perform this conversion step by step for the decimal number 999.\n\n1. Divide 999 by 6 to get the quotient and remainder.\n2. Divide the quotient from step 1 by 6 to get the next quotient and remainder.\n3. Repeat this process until the quotient is 0.\n4. The base six representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top.\n\nLet\'s perform these steps using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_six(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    base_six_digits = []\n    while n > 0:\n        remainder = n % 6\n        base_six_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        n = n // 6\n    \n    # The base six representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top\n    base_six_digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(base_six_digits)\n\n# Convert 999 from base 10 to base 6\nbase_six_representation = decimal_to_base_six(999)\nprint(base_six_representation)\n```\n```output\n4323\n```\nThe base six equivalent of \\(999_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{4323_6}\\).', 'To convert the decimal number \\(999_{10}\\) to its base six equivalent, we need to repeatedly divide the number by 6 and keep track of the remainders. The remainders, read in reverse order, will give 