bs(a + b - 1) - 1\nineq2_c2 = sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 - 1) - 1\n\n# Function to find the maximum value of |a| + |b| + |c|\ndef find_max_value(ineq1, ineq2):\n    # Consider the possible cases for the signs of the expressions inside the absolute values\n    cases = [\n        (ineq1.subs(sp.Abs(a + b + 1), a + b + 1), ineq2.subs(sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 + 1), a/4 + b/2 + 1)),\n        (ineq1.subs(sp.Abs(a + b + 1), a + b + 1), ineq2.subs(sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 + 1), -(a/4 + b/2 + 1))),\n        (ineq1.subs(sp.Abs(a + b + 1), -(a + b + 1)), ineq2.subs(sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 + 1), a/4 + b/2 + 1)),\n        (ineq1.subs(sp.Abs(a + b + 1), -(a + b + 1)), ineq2.subs(sp.Abs(a/4 + b/2 + 1), -(a/4 + b/2 + 1)))\n    ]\n    \n    max_value = 0\n    for case in cases:\n        # Solve the system of inequalities for a and b\n        solutions = sp.solve(case, (a, b), domain=sp.S.Reals)\n        for sol in solutions:\n            value = sp.Abs(sol[0]) + sp.Abs(sol[1]) + 1\n            if value > max_value:\n                max_value = value\n    \n    return max_value\n\n# Find the maximum value for c = 1 and c = -1\nmax_value_c1 = find_max_value(ineq1_c1, ineq2_c1)\nmax_value_c2 = find_max_value(ineq1_c2, ineq2_c2)\n\n# The overall maximum value is the maximum of the two cases\nmax_value = max(max_value_c1, max_value_c2)\n\nprint(max_value)\n```\n```output\nNotImplementedError: solving Abs(a + b + 1) when the argument is not real or imaginary.\n```\nIt seems that the direct approach using `sp.solve` with absolute values is still causing issues. Instead, we can manually handle the absolute value inequalities by considering the possible cases for the signs of the expressions inside the absolute values.\n\nLet's break down the problem into manageable steps and consider all possible cases for the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. **Evaluate at Key Points:**\n   - At \\(x = 0\\), \\(|c| \\le 1\\).\n   - At \\(x = 1\\), \\(|a + b + c| \\le 1\\).\n   - At \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), \\(\\left|\\frac{a}{