he hexagon is \\(9 - 1 = 8\\). The probability is the area of the hexagon divided by the area of the square, which is \\(\\frac{8}{9}\\).\n\nLet's write the final answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{18}}\n\\]", "To determine the probability that a triangle with sides of length 1, \\(x\\), and \\(y\\) exists, we need to use the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the following must be true:\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nIn our case, the sides of the triangle are 1, \\(x\\), and \\(y\\). So, the inequalities we need to satisfy are:\n\n1. \\(1 + x > y\\)\n2. \\(1 + y > x\\)\n3. \\(x + y > 1\\)\n\nSince \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are chosen from the interval \\((0, 3)\\), we can visualize the problem in the \\(xy\\)-plane. The region where \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) can lie is a square of side length 3, but we need to exclude the boundaries \\(x = 0\\), \\(x = 3\\), \\(y = 0\\), and \\(y = 3\\).\n\nThe inequalities \\(1 + x > y\\), \\(1 + y > x\\), and \\(x + y > 1\\) define a region within this square where the triangle can exist. We need to find the area of this region and divide it by the area of the entire square (which is \\(3 \\times 3 = 9\\)) to get the probability.\n\nLet's solve this step-by-step using Python and sympy:\n\n1. Define the inequalities.\n2. Find the area of the region defined by these inequalities within the square \\((0, 3) \\times (0, 3)\\).\n3. Calculate the probability as the ratio of this area to the area of the square.\n\nHere is the Python code to solve this:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, y = sp.symbols('x y')\n\n# Define the inequalities\nineq1 = sp.Heaviside(1 + x - y)\nineq2 = sp.Heaviside(1 + y - x)\nineq3 = sp.Heaviside(x + y - 1)\n\n# Define the region where the inequalities are satisfied\nregion = ineq1 * ineq2 * ineq3\n\n# Integrate the region over the square (0, 3) x (0, 3)\narea = sp.i