ive:\n\n\\[\n5(2x - 11) \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSince 5 is a positive constant, we can divide both sides of the inequality by 5 without changing the direction of the inequality:\n\n\\[\n2x - 11 \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nNext, solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n2x \\geq 11\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx \\geq \\frac{11}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx \\geq 5.5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest real number \\( x \\) in the domain of \\( g(x) \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5.5}\n\\]", "To find the smallest real number \\( x \\) in the domain of the function \\( g(x) = \\sqrt{(x-3)^2 - (x-8)^2} \\), we need to determine when the expression inside the square root is non-negative. That is, we need to solve the inequality:\n\n\\[\n(x-3)^2 - (x-8)^2 \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n(x-3)^2 - (x-8)^2 = (x^2 - 6x + 9) - (x^2 - 16x + 64) = x^2 - 6x + 9 - x^2 + 16x - 64 = 10x - 55.\n\\]\n\nSo, the inequality becomes:\n\n\\[\n10x - 55 \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n10x \\geq 55 \\implies x \\geq \\frac{55}{10} \\implies x \\geq 5.5.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest real number \\( x \\) in the domain of \\( g(x) \\) is \\( 5.5 \\). Let's confirm this by evaluating the function at \\( x = 5.5 \\) and ensuring it is defined.\n\nWe can use Python to verify this result.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variable and the function\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\nexpression = (x-3)**2 - (x-8)**2\r\n\r\n# Solve the inequality (x-3)^2 - (x-8)^2 >= 0\r\ninequality_solution = sp.solve(expression >= 0, x)\r\nprint(inequality_solution)\n```\n```output\n(11/2 <= x) & (x < oo)\n```\nThe solution to the inequality \\((x-3)^2 - (x-8)^2 \\geq 0\\) is \\( x \\geq \\frac{11}{2} \\), which is equivalent to \\( x \\geq 5.5 \\). This confirms that the smallest real number \\( x \\) in the domain of the function \\( g(x) = \\sqrt{(x-3)^2 - (x-8)^2} \\) is indeed \\( \\boxed{5.5} \\).", "To determine the smallest real number \\( x \\) in the domain of the function \\( g(x) = \\sqrt{(x-