5}x^2 - \\frac{33}{5}x + \\frac{23}{5}\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(P(16)\\):\n\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{9}{5}(16)^2 - \\frac{33}{5}(16) + \\frac{23}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{9}{5}(256) - \\frac{33}{5}(16) + \\frac{23}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{2304}{5} - \\frac{528}{5} + \\frac{23}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{2304 - 528 + 23}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{1800 - 528 + 23}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{1295}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = 259\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(P(16)\\) is \\(\\boxed{259}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the quadratic polynomial \\(P(x)\\) that satisfies the given inequalities and the condition \\(P(11) = 181\\). Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Define the quadratic polynomial \\(P(x)\\):**\n   Let \\(P(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\\).\n\n2. **Use the given inequalities:**\n   The inequalities \\(x^2 - 2x + 2 \\le P(x) \\le 2x^2 - 4x + 3\\) must hold for all real numbers \\(x\\). This implies that the polynomial \\(P(x)\\) must be a weighted average of the two boundary polynomials. Specifically, \\(P(x)\\) can be written as:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = k(x^2 - 2x + 2) + (1-k)(2x^2 - 4x + 3)\n   \\]\n   for some constant \\(k\\) between 0 and 1. Simplifying this expression, we get:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = (k \\cdot x^2 - 2k \\cdot x + 2k) + (2(1-k) \\cdot x^2 - 4(1-k) \\cdot x + 3(1-k))\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(x) = (k + 2(1-k))x^2 + (-2k - 4(1-k))x + (2k + 3(1-k))\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(x) = (2 - k)x^2 + (2k - 4)x + (3 - k)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the value of \\(k\\) using the condition \\(P(11) = 181\\):**\n   Substitute \\(x = 11\\) into \\(P(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   P(11) = (2 - k) \\cdot 11^2 + (2k - 4) \\cdot 11 + (3 - k) = 181\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (2 - k) \\cdot 121 + (2k - 4) \\cdot 11 + (3 - k) = 181\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   242 - 121k + 22k - 44 + 3 - k = 181\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   201 - 100k = 181\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   20 = 100k\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{1}{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find the coefficients of \\(P(x)\\) using \\(k = \\frac{1}{5}\\):**\n   Substitute \\(k 