3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4\\), and \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\). Let's denote these values as \\(S_1\\), \\(S_2\\), and \\(S_3\\), respectively. We know that:\n   \\[\n   S_1 + S_2 + S_3 = \\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 0\n   \\]\n   To find the individual values, we can use the fact that:\n   \\[\n   S_1 S_2 S_3 = (\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4)(\\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4)(\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3)\n   \\]\n   Expanding this product, we get:\n   \\[\n   S_1 S_2 S_3 = \\alpha_1^2 \\alpha_2 \\alpha_3 \\alpha_4 + \\alpha_1 \\alpha_2^2 \\alpha_3 \\alpha_4 + \\alpha_1 \\alpha_2 \\alpha_3^2 \\alpha_4 + \\alpha_1 \\alpha_2 \\alpha_3 \\alpha_4^2 + 2 \\alpha_1 \\alpha_2 \\alpha_3 \\alpha_4 (\\alpha_1 + \\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3 + \\alpha_4) + \\alpha_1^2 \\alpha_2^2 \\alpha_3^2 + \\alpha_1^2 \\alpha_2^2 \\alpha_4^2 + \\alpha_1^2 \\alpha_3^2 \\alpha_4^2 + \\alpha_2^2 \\alpha_3^2 \\alpha_4^2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying using Vieta's formulas, we get:\n   \\[\n   S_1 S_2 S_3 = 2(-2) + 2(2)(-2) + 2 = -4 - 8 + 2 = -10\n   \\]\n   We also know that:\n   \\[\n   S_1 + S_2 + S_3 = 0\n   \\]\n   The only possible values for \\(S_1, S_2, S_3\\) that satisfy these equations are the roots of the polynomial \\(t^3 - (S_1 + S_2 + S_3)t^2 + (S_1 S_2 + S_2 S_3 + S_3 S_1)t - S_1 S_2 S_3 = 0\\), which simplifies to \\(t^3 - 10 = 0\\). The roots of this polynomial are the cube roots of 10, which are \\(\\sqrt[3]{10}, \\sqrt[3]{10} \\omega, \\sqrt[3]{10} \\omega^2\\), where \\(\\omega\\) is a primitive cube root of unity.\n\n4. **Final answer**: The unordered set of the values is \\(\\{\\sqrt[3]{10}, \\sqrt[3]{10} \\omega, \\sqrt[3]{10} \\omega^2\\}\\). Since we are asked for the set, we can write the final answer as:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{-\\sqrt[3]{10}\\}}\n   \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define th