t to the parabola \\(y^2 = 8x\\) at the point \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is \\(yy_2 = 4(x + x_2)\\).\n\n3. **Finding Common Tangents:**\n   For the tangents to be common, the equations \\(xx_1 + yy_1 = 2\\) and \\(yy_2 = 4(x + x_2)\\) must represent the same line. This means that the coefficients must be proportional. So, we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x_1}{y_2/4} = \\frac{y_1}{4} = \\frac{2}{-y_2x_2}\n   \\]\n   From this, we get:\n   \\[\n   x_1 = \\frac{y_2}{4k}, \\quad y_1 = \\frac{4}{k}, \\quad 2 = -\\frac{y_2 x_2}{k}\n   \\]\n   where \\(k\\) is the proportionality constant. Since \\((x_1, y_1)\\) lies on the circle, we have:\n   \\[\n   x_1^2 + y_1^2 = 2 \\implies \\left(\\frac{y_2}{4k}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{4}{k}\\right)^2 = 2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y_2^2}{16k^2} + \\frac{16}{k^2} = 2 \\implies \\frac{y_2^2 + 256}{16k^2} = 2 \\implies y_2^2 + 256 = 32k^2\n   \\]\n   Since \\((x_2, y_2)\\) lies on the parabola, we have:\n   \\[\n   y_2^2 = 8x_2 \\implies x_2 = \\frac{y_2^2}{8}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x_2\\) into the equation \\(2 = -\\frac{y_2 x_2}{k}\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   2 = -\\frac{y_2 \\cdot \\frac{y_2^2}{8}}{k} = -\\frac{y_2^3}{8k} \\implies 16k = -y_2^3 \\implies k = -\\frac{y_2^3}{16}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(k\\) back into the equation \\(y_2^2 + 256 = 32k^2\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   y_2^2 + 256 = 32\\left(-\\frac{y_2^3}{16}\\right)^2 = 32 \\cdot \\frac{y_2^6}{256} = \\frac{y_2^6}{8}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8y_2^2 + 2048 = y_2^6\n   \\]\n   Solving this equation, we get \\(y_2 = \\pm 4\\). So, the points of tangency on the parabola are \\((2, 4)\\) and \\((2, -4)\\). The corresponding points on the circle are \\((-1, 1)\\) and \\((-1, -1)\\).\n\n4. **Area of the Quadrilateral:**\n   The vertices of the quadrilateral are \\((-1, 1)\\), \\((2, 4)\\), \\((-1, -1)\\), and \\((2, -4)\\). We can use the shoelace formula to find the area:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| -1 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot (-1) + (-1) \\cdo