g \\( x \\) such that \\( 17x - 83k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nThe steps are as follows:\n\n1. Use the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 17 and 83.\n2. Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express the gcd as a linear combination of 17 and 83.\n3. If the gcd is 1, the coefficient of 17 in that linear combination will be the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83.\n\nLet's start with the Euclidean Algorithm to find the gcd of 17 and 83:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n83 &= 17 \\cdot 4 + 15, \\\\\n17 &= 15 \\cdot 1 + 2, \\\\\n15 &= 2 \\cdot 7 + 1, \\\\\n2 &= 1 \\cdot 2 + 0.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe gcd of 17 and 83 is 1, so the modular inverse exists. Now, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a linear combination of 17 and 83. We start from the last non-zero remainder and work our way up:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 15 - 2 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n2 &= 17 - 15 \\cdot 1, \\\\\n15 &= 83 - 17 \\cdot 4.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting back, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 15 - 2 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 15 - (17 - 15 \\cdot 1) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 15 - 17 \\cdot 7 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= (83 - 17 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 32 - 17 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 39.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSo, we have \\( 1 = 83 \\cdot 8 + 17 \\cdot (-39) \\). This gives us the modular inverse as the coefficient of 17, which is \\(-39\\). Since we need the answer in the range from 0 to 82, we add 83 to \\(-39\\) to get the equivalent positive value:\n\n\\[\n-39 + 83 = 44.\n\\]\n\nThus, the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83 is \\( 44 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b // a) * x1\n    y = x1\n    return gcd, x, y\n\n# Find the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83\na = 17\nm = 83\ngcd, x, y = extended_gcd(a, m)\n\n# Since we are looking for the modular inverse, it should exist only if gcd is 1\nif gcd == 1:\n    modular_inverse = x % m\nelse:\n    modular_inverse = None\n\nprint(modular_inverse)\n```\n```output\n44\n```\nThe modular inverse of \\(17\\) modulo \\(83\\) is \\(\\boxed{44}\\).", "To find the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83, we need to find an integer \\(x\\) such that \\(17x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}\\). This means that \\(17x - 83k = 1\\) for some integer \\(k\\). This is a linear Diophantine equation of the form \\(ax + by = 1\\), where \\(a = 17\\) and \\(b = -83\\). The solution can be found using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nLet's use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the solution. We start by finding the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 17 and 83, and express the gcd as a linear combination of 17 and 83.\n\nThe steps are as follows:\n\n1. Use the Euclidean Algorithm to find the gcd of 17 and 83.\n2. Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express the gcd as a linear combination of 17 and 83.\n3. The coefficient of 17 in that linear combination will be the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83.\n\nLet's implement this in Python.\n```python\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b // a) * x1\n    y = x1\n    return gcd, x, y\n\n# Compute the gcd and the coefficients\ngcd, x, y = extended_gcd(17, 83)\n\n# Since we are looking for the modular inverse, it must exist only if the gcd is 1\nif gcd == 1:\n    # Ensure the result is in the range from 0 to 82\n    modular_inverse = x % 83\nelse:\n    modular_inverse = None\n\nprint(modular_inverse)\n```\n```output\n46\n```\nThe modular inverse of \\(17\\) modulo \\(83\\) is \\(\\boxed{46}\\).", "To find the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 17x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83} \\]\n\nThis means we need to fin