ot 1 + 2, \\\\\n15 &= 2 \\cdot 7 + 1, \\\\\n2 &= 1 \\cdot 2 + 0.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe gcd of 17 and 83 is 1, which means that the inverse exists. Now, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a linear combination of 17 and 83:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 15 - 2 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n2 &= 17 - 15 \\cdot 1, \\\\\n15 &= 83 - 17 \\cdot 4.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting back, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 15 - 2 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n1 &= 15 - (17 - 15 \\cdot 1) \\cdot 7, \\\\\n1 &= 15 - 17 \\cdot 7 + 15 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n1 &= 15 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n1 &= (83 - 17 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n1 &= 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 32 - 17 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n1 &= 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 39.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have $17 \\cdot (-39) + 83 \\cdot 8 = 1$. The coefficient of 17 is $-39$, so the inverse of 17 modulo 83 is $-39$. Since we want the answer to be in the range from 0 to 82, we add 83 to $-39$ to get a positive equivalent:\n\n\\[\n-39 + 83 = 44.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of 17 modulo 83 is 44. Let's verify this by checking that $17 \\cdot 44 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}$.\n\n\\[\n17 \\cdot 44 = 748 = 9 \\cdot 83 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}.\n\\]\n\nThe calculation confirms that the inverse is correct. So, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{44}\n\\]\n\nLet's also verify this using Python code.\n```python\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b // a) * x1\n    y = x1\n    return gcd, x, y\n\na = 17\nb = 83\ngcd, x, y = extended_gcd(a, b)\ninverse = x % b  # Ensure the result is in the range from 0 to b-1\nprint(inverse)\n```", 'To solve for $17^{-1} \\pmod{83}$, we need to find an integer $x$ such that $17x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}$. This is equivalent to finding the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83. One way to find the modular inverse is to use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, which finds solutions to equations of the form $ax + by = \\gc