m to find this inverse.\n\nUsing the Extended Euclidean Algorithm:\n\n\\[50 = 7 \\cdot 7 + 1,\\]\n\\[1 = 50 - 7 \\cdot 7.\\]\n\nThis shows that the inverse of 7 modulo 50 is \\(-7\\). Since we want a positive inverse, we take \\(-7 + 50 = 43\\). So, the inverse of 7 modulo 50 is 43. Now we can solve for \\(u\\):\n\n\\[u \\equiv 23 \\cdot 43 \\pmod{50}.\\]\n\nWe can calculate \\(23 \\cdot 43 \\mod 50\\):\n\n\\[23 \\cdot 43 = 989,\\]\n\\[989 \\mod 50 = 39.\\]\n\nSo, \\(u \\equiv 39 \\pmod{50}\\). This means that the general solution for \\(u\\) is \\(u = 39 + 50k\\) for any integer \\(k\\). The two smallest positive integer solutions are when \\(k = 0\\) and \\(k = 1\\), giving us \\(u = 39\\) and \\(u = 89\\).\n\nThe average of these two smallest positive integer solutions is:\n\n\\[\\frac{39 + 89}{2} = \\frac{128}{2} = 64.\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{64}.\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Function to find the modular inverse using Extended Euclidean Algorithm\r\ndef modular_inverse(a, m):\r\n    m0, x0, x1 = m, 0, 1\r\n    if m == 1:\r\n        return 0\r\n    while a > 1:\r\n        # q is quotient\r\n        q = a // m\r\n        m, a = a % m, m\r\n        x0, x1 = x1 - q * x0, x0\r\n    # Make x1 positive\r\n    if x1 < 0:\r\n        x1 += m0\r\n    return x1\r\n\r\n# Given values\r\na = 14\r\nb = 46\r\nm = 100\r\n\r\n# Simplify the congruence by dividing by gcd(14, 100) = 2\r\na_simplified = a // 2\r\nb_simplified = b // 2\r\nm_simplified = m // 2\r\n\r\n# Find the modular inverse of 7 modulo 50\r\ninverse = modular_inverse(a_simplified, m_simplified)\r\n\r\n# Solve for u\r\nu = (b_simplified * inverse) % m_simplified\r\n\r\n# The general solution is u = u + 50k, we need the two smallest positive solutions\r\nu1 = u\r\nu2 = u + m_simplified\r\n\r\n# Calculate the average of the two smallest positive integer solutions\r\naverage = (u1 + u2) // 2\r\nprint(average)\n```", "To solve the congr