$.\n\nSince $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, the corresponding angles and alternate interior angles will be equal. We can use the fact that the sum of angles in a triangle is $180^\\circ$ to find the measure of $\\angle STR$.\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\angle S$. Since $\\angle P$ and $\\angle Q$ are given, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in $\\triangle PQS$ is $180^\\circ$. So, $\\angle S = 180^\\circ - \\angle P - \\angle Q = 180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 105^\\circ$.\n\nSince $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, $\\angle STR$ is equal to $\\angle S$ because they are corresponding angles. Therefore, $\\angle STR = \\angle S = 105^\\circ$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Calculate angle S in triangle PQS\nangle_S = 180 - angle_P - angle_Q\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle S\nangle_STR = angle_S\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n105\n```\nThe measure of $\\angle STR$ is \\(\\boxed{105}\\) degrees.", 'Since $\\overline{ST}\\parallel\\overline{QR}$, we have $\\angle STR=\\angle Q$.  Therefore, $\\angle STR=\\boxed{35^\\circ}$.', "To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of parallel lines and angles. Given that $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, we know that $\\angle STR$ is equal to $\\angle Q$ because they are corresponding angles. \n\nHere is the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Since $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, the angle $\\angle STR$ is equal to $\\angle Q$.\n2. Given that $\\angle Q = 35^\\circ$, we can directly conclude that $\\angle STR = 35^\\circ$.\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code using sympy to ensure the result is accurate.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle Q\nangle_STR = angle_Q\n\n# Print the result\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\