n   - The probability of obtaining each of these pairs is the product of the probabilities of the individual faces.\n   - The probability of obtaining a sum of 7 is given by:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{sum of 7}) = 2pq + 4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\right) = \\frac{47}{288}\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     2pq + \\frac{4}{36} = \\frac{47}{288}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2pq + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{47}{288}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2pq = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{1}{9}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2pq = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{32}{288} = \\frac{15}{288} = \\frac{5}{96}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     pq = \\frac{5}{192}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Solve for $p$ and $q$:**\n   - We have the equations:\n     \\[\n     p + q = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     pq = \\frac{5}{192}\n     \\]\n   - These are the sum and product of the roots of a quadratic equation. The quadratic equation is:\n     \\[\n     x^2 - \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)x + \\frac{5}{192} = 0\n     \\]\n   - Solving this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula $x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, we get:\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 - 4 \\cdot \\frac{5}{192}}}{2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{5}{48}}}{2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{144} - \\frac{15}{144}}}{2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{144}}}{2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pm \\frac{1}{12}}{2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\frac{4}{12} \\pm \\frac{1}{12}}{2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{\\frac{5}{12}}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = \\frac{\\frac{3}{12}}{2}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{5}{24} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = \\frac{1}{8}\n     \\]\n   - Since $p > \\frac{1}{6}$ and $q < \\frac{1}{6}$, we have $p = \\frac{5}{24}$ and $q = \\frac{1}{8}$.\n\n5. **Find $m$ and $n$: