te their total distances. This is essentially finding the shortest Hamiltonian path in a weighted graph.\n\nLet\'s list all possible routes and their distances:\n\n1. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\)\n2. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\)\n3. \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\)\n4. \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\)\n5. \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\)\n6. \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\)\n\nWe\'ll calculate the distance for each route:\n\n1. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 4 + 5 = 15\\)\n2. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\): \\(6 + 5 + 4 = 15\\)\n3. \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 4 + 5 = 15\\)\n4. \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\): \\(6 + 5 + 5 = 16\\)\n5. \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\): \\(5 + 5 + 4 = 14\\)\n6. \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\): \\(5 + 4 + 4 = 13\\)\n\nThe shortest distance is the minimum of these distances. Let\'s use Python to verify this.\n```python\n# Define the distances between the points\ndistances = {\n    (\'A\', \'B\'): 6,\n    (\'A\', \'C\'): 6,\n    (\'A\', \'D\'): 5,\n    (\'B\', \'C\'): 4,\n    (\'B\', \'D\'): 5,\n    (\'C\', \'D\'): 5\n}\n\n# Define all possible routes\nroutes = [\n    [\'A\', \'B\', \'C\', \'D\'],\n    [\'A\', \'B\', \'D\', \'C\'],\n    [\'A\', \'C\', \'B\', \'D\'],\n    [\'A\', \'C\', \'D\', \'B\'],\n    [\'A\', \'D\', \'B\', \'C\'],\n    [\'A\', \'D\', \'C\', \'B\']\n]\n\n# Function to calculate the total distance of a route\ndef calculate_distance(route):\n    total_distance = 0\n    for i in range(len(route) - 1):\n        total_distance += distances[(route[i], route[i + 1])]\n    return total_distance\n\n# Calculate the distance for each route and find the minimum\nmin_distance = float(\'inf\')\nfor route in routes:\n    distance = calculate_distance(route)\n    i