s (x - r_n)\\]\n\nwhere \\(r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n\\) are distinct integers. The constant term \\(a_0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[a_0 = 2(-r_1)(-r_2) \\cdots (-r_n) = 2r_1r_2 \\cdots r_n = 66\\]\n\nSo, we need to find distinct integers \\(r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n\\) such that their product is \\(66 / 2 = 33\\). The prime factorization of 33 is \\(33 = 3 \\cdot 11\\). The possible sets of distinct integer factors of 33 are \\(\\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11\\}\\).\n\nTo find the least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\), we need to consider the sum of the roots taken one at a time, which is given by:\n\n\\[a_{n-1} = -2(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n)\\]\n\nWe need to find the combination of roots that minimizes \\(|a_{n-1}|\\). Let's consider the possible combinations of roots and calculate \\(|a_{n-1}|\\):\n\n1. If the roots are \\(\\{1, 3, 11\\}\\), then \\(|a_{n-1}| = |-2(1 + 3 + 11)| = |-2 \\cdot 15| = 30\\).\n2. If the roots are \\(\\{-1, -3, -11\\}\\), then \\(|a_{n-1}| = |-2(-1 - 3 - 11)| = |-2 \\cdot (-15)| = 30\\).\n3. If the roots are \\(\\{1, -3, -11\\}\\), then \\(|a_{n-1}| = |-2(1 - 3 - 11)| = |-2 \\cdot (-13)| = 26\\).\n4. If the roots are \\(\\{-1, 3, 11\\}\\), then \\(|a_{n-1}| = |-2(-1 + 3 + 11)| = |-2 \\cdot 13| = 26\\).\n5. If the roots are \\(\\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11\\}\\), then \\(|a_{n-1}| = |-2(1 - 1 + 3 - 3 + 11 - 11)| = |-2 \\cdot 0| = 0\\).\n\nThe least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is 0. Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\n# Possible roots that multiply to 33\npossible_roots = [1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11]\n\n# Function to calculate |a_{n-1}|\ndef calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots):\n    return abs(-2 * sum(roots))\n\n# Find the minimum |a_{n-1}|\nmin_abs_a_n_minus_1 = float('inf')\nbest_combination = None\n\n# Check all combinations of roots\nfor r in range(1, len(possible_roots) + 1):\n    for combo in combinations(possible_roots, r):\n        if abs(np.prod(combo)) == 33:\n            abs_a_n_minus_1