1} = -2 \\cdot 0 = 0\\), and \\(|a_{n-1}| = 0\\).\n\n2. For \\(\\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11\\}\\):\n\\[ 1 - 1 + 3 - 3 + 11 = 11. \\]\nSo, \\(a_{n-1} = -2 \\cdot 11 = -22\\), and \\(|a_{n-1}| = 22\\).\n\n3. For \\(\\{1, -1, 3, -3\\}\\):\n\\[ 1 - 1 + 3 - 3 = 0. \\]\nSo, \\(a_{n-1} = -2 \\cdot 0 = 0\\), and \\(|a_{n-1}| = 0\\).\n\n4. For \\(\\{1, -1, 33\\}\\):\n\\[ 1 - 1 + 33 = 33. \\]\nSo, \\(a_{n-1} = -2 \\cdot 33 = -66\\), and \\(|a_{n-1}| = 66\\).\n\n5. For \\(\\{1, -33\\}\\):\n\\[ 1 - 33 = -32. \\]\nSo, \\(a_{n-1} = -2 \\cdot (-32) = 64\\), and \\(|a_{n-1}| = 64\\).\n\nThe smallest possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is \\(0\\). Therefore, the least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "Given the polynomial \\( f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66 \\) with integer coefficients and distinct integer roots, we need to find the least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nFirst, let's denote the roots of the polynomial by \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\). Since the polynomial has integer coefficients and its roots are distinct integers, we can express the polynomial as:\n\\[\nf(x) = 2(x - r_1)(x - r_2) \\cdots (x - r_n)\n\\]\n\nThe constant term \\( a_0 \\) of the polynomial is given by:\n\\[\na_0 = 2(-r_1)(-r_2) \\cdots (-r_n) = 2r_1r_2 \\cdots r_n\n\\]\nGiven \\( a_0 = 66 \\), we have:\n\\[\n2r_1r_2 \\cdots r_n = 66\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\nr_1r_2 \\cdots r_n = 33\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the distinct integer roots \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\) such that their product is 33. The possible sets of distinct integer factors of 33 are:\n\\[\n\\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 33, -33\\}\n\\]\n\nWe will consider different combinations of these factors to find the one that minimizes \\( |a_{n-1}| \\). The coefficient \\( a_{n-1} \\) is given by the sum of the roots taken one at a time, multiplied by the leading coefficient:\n\\[\na_{n-1} = 2 \\sum_{i=1}^n r_i\n\\]\n\nLet's evaluate \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) for different combinations of roots:\n\n1. **Combinat