, -32, 32, -8, 8$. The absolute values are $34, 34, 14, 14, 32, 32, 8, 8$.\n2. If we have 3 roots, the possible triplets are $(1, 3, 11)$, $(-1, -3, -11)$, $(1, 3, -11)$, $(-1, -3, 11)$, $(1, -3, 11)$, $(-1, 3, -11)$, $(1, -3, -11)$, $(-1, 3, 11)$. The sums of these triplets are $15, -15, -7, 7, 9, -9, -13, 13$. The absolute values are $15, 15, 7, 7, 9, 9, 13, 13$.\n3. If we have 4 roots, the possible quadruplets are $(1, 3, 11, -1)$, $(-1, -3, -11, 1)$, $(1, 3, -11, -1)$, $(-1, -3, 11, 1)$, $(1, -3, 11, -1)$, $(-1, 3, -11, 1)$, $(1, -3, -11, 1)$, $(-1, 3, 11, -1)$. The sums of these quadruplets are $4, -4, -8, 8, 0, 0, -2, 2$. The absolute values are $4, 4, 8, 8, 0, 0, 2, 2$.\n\nFrom the above calculations, the smallest absolute value we get is 2. Therefore, the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$ is 2. Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Possible roots\nroots = [1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 33, -33]\n\n# Function to find the minimum absolute value of a_{n-1}\ndef find_min_abs_a_n_minus_1(roots):\n    min_abs_sum = float('inf')\n    # Check all combinations of roots\n    for r in range(2, len(roots) + 1):\n        for combination in itertools.combinations(roots, r):\n            # Calculate the sum of the roots\n            sum_of_roots = sum(combination)\n            # Calculate the absolute value of the sum\n            abs_sum = abs(sum_of_roots)\n            # Update the minimum absolute value if a smaller one is found\n            if abs_sum < min_abs_sum:\n                min_abs_sum = abs_sum\n    return min_abs_sum\n\n# Find the minimum absolute value of a_{n-1}\nmin_abs_a_n_minus_1 = find_min_abs_a_n_minus_1(roots)\nprint(min_abs_a_n_minus_1)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt appears that the code returned 0 as the minimum absolute value of $a_{n-1}$. This suggests that there is a combination of roots that sums to 0. Let's verify this manually:\n\nConsider the roots $1, -1, 3, -3$. The sum of these roots is $1 + (-1