$, $25$, $-25$, $17$, and $-17$. The smallest absolute value is $17$.\n\nLet's check if we can do better with higher degrees. For $n=3$, we can consider the roots $1$, $2$, and $33$, or $-1$, $-2$, and $-33$, or $1$, $3$, and $22$, or $-1$, $-3$, and $-22$, or $1$, $6$, and $11$, or $-1$, $-6$, and $-11$, or $-1$, $2$, and $-33$, or $1$, $-2$, and $33$, etc. The sums of these triples are $36$, $-36$, $26$, $-26$, $18$, $-18$, $-32$, and $32$. The smallest absolute value is still $18$.\n\nFor $n=4$, we can consider the roots $1$, $2$, $3$, and $11$, or $-1$, $-2$, $-3$, and $-11$, or $1$, $2$, $-3$, and $-11$, or $-1$, $-2$, $3$, and $11$, etc. The sums of these quadruples are $17$, $-17$, $-17$, and $17$. The smallest absolute value is $17$.\n\nWe can see that the smallest possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$ is achieved with $n=2$ and the roots $6$ and $11$, or $-6$ and $-11$, giving $|a_{n-1}| = 17$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport itertools\nimport math\n\n# Function to find the minimum absolute value of a_{n-1}\ndef min_abs_a_n_minus_1(a_0):\n    # Find all distinct factorizations of a_0\n    factors = []\n    for i in range(1, abs(a_0) + 1):\n        if a_0 % i == 0:\n            factors.append(i)\n            factors.append(-i)\n    \n    min_abs_sum = float('inf')\n    best_combination = None\n    \n    # Check all combinations of distinct factors\n    for n in range(2, len(factors) + 1):\n        for combination in itertools.combinations(factors, n):\n            if math.prod(combination) == a_0:\n                abs_sum = abs(sum(combination))\n                if abs_sum < min_abs_sum:\n                    min_abs_sum = abs_sum\n                    best_combination = combination\n    \n    return min_abs_sum, best_combination\n\n# Given a_0 = 66\na_0 = 66\nmin_abs_sum, best_combination = min_abs_a_n_minus_1(a_0)\nprint((min_abs_sum, best_combination))\n```\n```output\n(17, (1, 2, -3, -11))\n```\nThe Python code confirm