the product of primes starting from 11. The smallest such composite number is $11 \\times 11 = 121$. The next such number would be the next smallest product of primes starting from 11, which is $11 \\times 13 = 143$.\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure accuracy. We'll first write a function to check if a number has any prime factors less than 10, and then we'll iterate to find the next number that meets the criteria.\n```python\ndef has_prime_factors_less_than_10(n):\n    # Check if the number has any prime factors less than 10\n    for prime in [2, 3, 5, 7]:\n        if n % prime == 0:\n            return True\n    return False\n\n# The smallest composite number with no prime factors less than 10 is 121\n# Now we need to find the next such number\nnext_number = 122\nwhile True:\n    if not has_prime_factors_less_than_10(next_number):\n        break\n    next_number += 1\n\nprint(next_number)\n```\n```output\n127\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the logic. The number 127 is a prime number, not a composite number. We need to ensure that the number is composite. Let's correct the code to ensure that we only consider composite numbers.\n```python\ndef is_composite(n):\n    # Check if the number is composite\n    if n <= 3:\n        return False\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        return True\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return True\n        i += 6\n    return False\n\ndef has_prime_factors_less_than_10(n):\n    # Check if the number has any prime factors less than 10\n    for prime in [2, 3, 5, 7]:\n        if n % prime == 0:\n            return True\n    return False\n\n# The smallest composite number with no prime factors less than 10 is 121\n# Now we need to find the next such number\nnext_number = 122\nwhile True:\n    if is_composite(next_number) and not has_prime_factors_less_than_10(next_number):\n        break\n    next_number += 1\n\nprint(next_number)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe next smallest composite number with no prime factors less than 10 is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).\n\nA certain number of men can finish a piece of work in 100 days at a standard work rate. If there were 10 men less, it would take 10 days more for the work to be finished. How many men were there originally?\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine the original number of men required to complete the work in 100 days. Let's denote the original number of men by \\( m \\).\n\nThe amount of work done by the men can be expressed in terms of man-days. If \\( m \\) men can complete the work in 100 days, then the total amount of work is \\( m \\times 100 \\) man-days.\n\nIf there are 10 men less, the number of men is \\( m - 10 \\), and it takes 110 days to complete the same work. So the total amount of work can also be expressed as \\( (m - 10) \\times 110 \\) man-days.\n\nSince the total amount of work is the same in both cases, we can set up the following equation:\n\\[ m \\times 100 = (m - 10) \\times 110 \\]\n\nNow we can solve this equation for \\( m \\):\n\n\\[ 100m = 110(m - 10) \\]\n\\[ 100m = 110m - 1100 \\]\n\\[ 1100 = 110m - 100m \\]\n\\[ 1100 = 10m \\]\n\\[ m = \\frac{1100}{10} \\]\n\\[ m = 110 \\]\n\nSo, the original number of men is \\( 110 \\). Let's verify this using Python code to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\n# Define the original number of men and the days taken\nm = 110\ndays_original = 100\ndays_reduced = days_original + 10\nmen_reduced = m - 10\n\n# Calculate the total work in man-days for both scenarios\nwork_original = m * days_original\nwork_reduced = men_reduced * days_reduced\n\n# Verify if both calculations give the same amount of work\nprint((work_original, work_reduced))\n```\n```output\n(11000, 11000)\n```\nThe calculations confirm that the total amount of work is the same in both scenarios, with \\( 11000 \\) man-days of work. This verifies that our solution is correct.\n\nSo, the original number of men was \\(\\boxed{110}\